# Collapse the old Netlify subdomain onto the real domain.
#
# soft-nasturtium-c51aac.netlify.app was serving a full 200 copy of the app, not a
# redirect. Two live origins is not a cosmetic problem here: a Supabase session
# lives in one origin's localStorage, so a performer who finished Stripe onboarding
# and got returned to the subdomain landed on a LOGGED OUT copy. stripe-status never
# ran, readiness never synced, and they showed up to a gig unable to take tips with
# no error anywhere. stripe-onboard now sends everyone to getliveque.com; this closes
# the other half by making the subdomain unreachable as a place to be logged in.
#
# The trailing ! forces the redirect even where a matching file exists, which is
# required here because every one of these paths does exist on the subdomain.

https://soft-nasturtium-c51aac.netlify.app/*  https://getliveque.com/:splat  301!
http://soft-nasturtium-c51aac.netlify.app/*   https://getliveque.com/:splat  301!

# landing.html is the old standalone front page, superseded when the landing copy
# moved into index.html. It was left live in case Glen had pasted the link
# somewhere, and a redirect keeps that promise while removing the duplicate.
#
# It is not merely redundant, it is wrong: it still advertises "Apple Pay, Google
# Pay, a card, or your Venmo". Venmo was deliberately removed as a tip option
# because Venmo prohibits personal accounts from accepting business payments and
# performers risked having funds frozen. That page is live on the domain Stripe is
# about to review for activation.
/landing.html  /  301!

# archive/ holds dated snapshots of old builds. Netlify publishes everything in the
# repo root, so those were being served live: three separate older versions of the
# app reachable on the production domain, each still carrying the Venmo / PayPal /
# CashApp / Zelle payment-settings UI that was removed on purpose.
#
# Scanned before writing this: they contain no secret keys, only the Supabase anon
# JWT, which is public by design. So this is duplicate content and a bad look during
# Stripe review, not a credential leak. Keep them in git, stop serving them.
/archive/*  /  301!
